# GitHub issue fixes

When implementing a fix for a numbered GitHub issue:

- **Do not** run `gh issue close` or mark the issue complete in comments unless the user explicitly asks you to close it.
- **Do** implement the fix, run tests, and briefly tell the user which issue was addressed and how to verify.
- The user closes the issue after they test on their machine or the NUC.

When the user asks to close a **parent issue** that has sub-issues (e.g. [#30](https://github.com/rfreedman/dvra-repeater-announcements/issues/30), [#31](https://github.com/rfreedman/dvra-repeater-announcements/issues/31), [#32](https://github.com/rfreedman/dvra-repeater-announcements/issues/32)), close **all linked sub-issues in the same turn**, then the parent, unless they say otherwise.

GitHub is the tracker for bugs and minor enhancements. Do not copy those into repo docs or cursor rules.

When you create a GitHub issue, delete any local note that only describes that bug or minor enhancement.

Keep local docs for major future or optional work (design notes, operator behavior, hardware plans) that is not a filed bug or small enhancement.
